=== Relationship to the sbm ADT ===

The posting-list ("value is a set of locators") paradigm rests on a compact,
sorted, mergeable integer-set representation. That is the '''sbm''' ADT
(sparse bitmap set, <code>src/backend/lib/sbm.c</code> +
<code>src/include/lib/sbm.h</code>), vendored from the sparsemap library and
committed ahead of the AM as a standalone, independently tested helper (model:
<code>lib/integerset</code>, not a <code>Node</code> type).

sbm's measured envelope drives where the posting-list shape pays off:

* As a raw TID posting list of uniformly random TIDs it costs about 24 bytes
  per TID -- ~4x worse than the raw 6-byte ItemPointer -- so BARK '''must not'''
  encode a small or scattered dup set as an sbm.
* For a '''clustered / low-cardinality''' dup set (the case an inverted index
  actually produces: a hot trigram, a common attribute value) it costs
  0.2-1.9 bytes per TID and its set-algebra (union/intersect for
  BitmapAnd/BitmapOr) runs in O(chunks), not O(TIDs).

So the AM chooses the encoding per posting list by density, and sbm is the
substrate for the clustered case only. sbm's build path is O(N log N) through
<code>sbm_add_many_grow</code> (sort + threaded cursor); a naive per-element
random insert loop is O(N^2) and BARK must route bulk posting-list
construction through the bulk entry point.

== The six re-federation contracts ==

nbtree is not "just an AM": twenty-odd places in the backend assume the only
ordered index is a B-tree and the only row identifier is a heap TID. Before
BARK can be a peer of nbtree, those assumptions have to be named as contracts
that either side can declare and the dependent code can test. Five of the six
contracts come from other patch sets; the sixth is the sweep that removes the hardcodes. KNN
(<code>amcanorderbyop</code>) is reserved in the API from day one and
implemented later.

=== C-LOCATOR: what an index entry points at ===

''Interface: <code>src/include/access/amlocator.h</code>.''

An index entry stores a '''locator''': the value it hands the table AM to
identify a row. Today every table AM hands out an <code>ItemPointerData</code>
and every index AM stores one, so the contract holds by convention and is
never checked. Correctness of bitmap scans, index-scan prefetch, and TID-range
scans each rests on a specific property of that locator.

The contract has the table AM describe its locator with a
<code>LocatorDesc</code>, returned per relation by the
<code>TableAmRoutine.relation_locator</code> callback and read through
<code>RelationGetLocatorDesc()</code> (an AM that leaves the callback NULL is
described as heap is):

* <code>width</code> -- size in bytes; a negative value is the largest size of
  a variable-width locator;
* <code>max_offset</code> -- the largest item number any locator carries
  (heap: <code>MaxHeapTuplesPerPage</code>). Above
  <code>TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE</code> the planner builds no bitmap scan of
  the table and GIN refuses to index it;
* <code>name</code> -- for error messages;
* <code>stable</code> -- a row keeps its locator for its whole life (heap:
  false; an in-place-update AM: true);
* <code>old_version_retained</code> -- the pre-update version stays fetchable
  through its locator after the UPDATE (heap: true; an in-place AM says false,
  and the executor copies the old row before the write, see
  <code>RelationUpdatesInPlace()</code>);
* <code>bitmap_and_inexact</code>, <code>bitmap_or_inexact</code> -- table-AM
  callbacks that flag the bitmap groups a BitmapAnd or BitmapOr cannot combine
  exactly; the combine then unions the group's slots and marks it for recheck
  (NULL: every group is exact).

'''AM-routine wiring:''' the index side declares one flag,
<code>IndexAmRoutine.amcanvarlocator</code> (<code>can_var_locator</code> in
<code>pg_indexam_has_property</code>). An AM that leaves it false stores the
locator in an <code>ItemPointerData</code>, and
<code>index_check_locator()</code> -- called by <code>index_create()</code>
and again by <code>index_build()</code>, since a table's AM can change under
its indexes -- rejects such an index unless the table's locator is
fixed-width and no wider than 6 bytes.

'''BARK obligation:''' leave <code>amcanvarlocator</code> false unless the
format can store a wider locator; refuse, at build, a table whose descriptor
promises more than BARK's entry shapes can encode; never assume more of the
locator than the descriptor states. ''Status:'' BARK leaves
<code>amcanvarlocator</code> false, because its format stores a fixed 6-byte
<code>ItemPointerData</code>: SINGLE entries keep the locator in
<code>t_tid</code>, LIST entries are arrays of it (a coded LIST packs the
offset into <code>max(11, bits of the stride)</code> bits), and POSTING
entries map a TID to the 64-bit key <code>block * stride + offset - 1</code>,
where the stride is the table's <code>max_offset</code> at CREATE INDEX,
kept in the meta page (<code>bark_tid_stride</code>).
<code>index_check_locator()</code> therefore admits a BARK index only on a
table whose locator is fixed-width and fits in 6 bytes; a larger
<code>max_offset</code> (up to <code>MaxOffsetNumber</code>) is indexed, with
the mappings laid out for it. <code>ginbuild()</code> refuses such a table.

''As built (2026-10-20, TID stride).'' Phase 2 first refused, in
<code>bark_build()</code>, a table whose <code>max_offset</code> exceeds
<code>MaxHeapTuplesPerPage</code>; the refusal is gone. CREATE INDEX stores
the table's <code>max_offset</code> as the index's TID stride in what was
the padding after <code>bark_allequalimage</code>, so a heap index's pages
are unchanged and an older meta page reads 0, meaning
<code>MaxHeapTuplesPerPage</code>; no version change. Every TID mapping takes
the stride: the POSTING key, the coded LIST (heap's 11 offset bits are
GIN's; MaxOffsetNumber at BLCKSZ 8192 takes 12), and the scan seen set's
radix-tree key. ADD_TID and INSERT_SWAP redo recompute their entry from the
logged TID, so a record of an index whose stride is not heap's carries it
after its struct. aminsert and the build callback refuse a TID past the
stride (the table's locators changed without a rebuild). A GIN-interface
index scan, whose result is a TIDBitmap of BARK's own, folds an offset past
<code>TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE</code> into further bitmap pages of the same
heap block, one-to-one and in TID order; the executor's bitmaps are not
folded, since the planner makes no bitmap scan of such a table. REINDEX and
ALTER TABLE ... SET ACCESS METHOD rebuild with the new table's stride. Tests:
<code>bark_locator.sql</code> (the stride through CREATE INDEX [CONCURRENTLY],
SET ACCESS METHOD both ways and REINDEX; the mappings on synthetic TIDs at
291 and MaxOffsetNumber, offsets 291/292 and 2047/2048, blocks 0 and
MaxBlockNumber; the insert guard), <code>bark_wideblock.sql</code> on
<code>bark_test_wideblock</code>, a heap whose TIDs fold seven blocks into
one, so offsets reach 2037 (builds, every entry shape, every scan, VACUUM,
bottom-up deletion, CIC, each compared with a seqscan and checked by
<code>bark_index_check</code>), and <code>t/002_wideblock_recovery.pl</code>
(standby replay under <code>wal_consistency_checking</code>, and crash
recovery).
The other fields ask nothing of an index AM: <code>stable</code> and
<code>old_version_retained</code> are the executor's, and the bitmap
callbacks are the table AM's. A wider locator would need a format change;
none is planned.

==== Versioned locators: BARK stores version tags ====

''Design: <code>DESIGN-split-and-hot.md</code> sections 2, 5, 6.5b, 6.6
and 8b; interface: "Versioned locators" in <code>amlocator.h</code>;
details: "Version tags" in the README.''

A table AM whose <code>LocatorDesc.versioned</code> is set hands each index
entry a 4-byte version tag, and an entry names a row version as
(key, TID, tag), 0 meaning untagged. The table AM decides whether an entry
is valid for the version a snapshot sees; BARK stores, orders and returns
tags and never interprets them.

''As built (phase 5b, 2026-10-22).''
* '''Format version 3.''' A tagged SINGLE sets the alt-TID bit with
  <code>BARK_HAS_TAG</code> (0x4000, once reserved for delete-marking) and
  keeps heap TID then tag in a MAXALIGNed trailer after its key; a tagged
  OVERSIZED entry keeps its tag in an 8-byte trailer after its ref (the ref
  had no free bytes, against the design's plan). LIST and POSTING entries
  are never tagged: tagged entries never coalesce. Untagged entries are byte
  for byte as in version 2, which is read and written as it is.
* '''Order and pivots.''' Entries are ordered by (key, TID, tag), the tag
  as a raw uint32. A pivot keeps a tag only between two entries of one row
  equal on key and TID (<code>BARK_PIVOT_TAG</code>). The pivot suffix
  reserve is 16 bytes only in an index built for a versioned table
  (<code>BARK_META_VERSIONED</code>), so indexes of heap tables keep their
  pages exactly.
* '''Per index.''' <code>amindexcanversion</code>: an index stores tags
  only with <code>BARK_META_VERSIONED</code> and no extracted column. A
  multikey index returns each row once by TID, which per-entry tags would
  break, so the executor moves a row whenever an update affects it.
* '''Writers and readers.''' aminsert and the build read
  <code>ii_VersionTag</code>; a tagged row's entry divides an untagged LIST
  or POSTING whose TID range it falls in. Every amgettuple path returns the
  entry's tag in <code>xs_heaptag</code>; a bitmap scan drops it. The unique
  check asks <code>table_fetch_tid_version</code> with the entry's tag;
  <code>UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING</code> recognizes the row's own entries by
  TID. An LP_DEAD kill marks only the entry of the tag returned. A
  (K, L, t) the index already has is not stored again (2026-10-27): a
  table AM may give several versions of a row one tag
  (heap_versioned uses the transaction ID), so A -> B -> A, or a key two
  in-place updates keep, re-inserts an entry; a second copy broke the
  strict (key, TID, tag) order and made an index scan return the row
  twice. An insert that meets its copy marked LP_DEAD clears the mark.
* '''VACUUM and the untag rule (R3.2).''' VACUUM asks the table AM's
  version callback about every entry. LIVE_UNTAG moves an entry from
  (K, L, t) to (K, L, 0), before every entry of (K, L) with a tag. BARK
  moves it only within its page, in the page's VACUUM record, and only when
  nothing on another page can lie above (K, L, 0): an entry before its slot
  on the page, a leftmost page, or a page without
  <code>BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED</code>. That flag marks a leaf whose lower
  bound may have a tag. A split sets it on its right half when the high key
  keeps a tag, and page deletion hands it to the right sibling. An entry
  that cannot move is kept tagged (LIVE) through the scan, and VACUUM then
  lowers the bound (T2, below) and untags it, or counts it at DEBUG2. An
  entry whose (K, L, 0) is the row's own untagged entry also stays tagged
  and is counted (T3, 2026-10-24): under the per-index validity rule a
  correct table AM has found that entry DEAD in the same pass, so the count
  is 0 unless the AM answered LIVE for an invalid entry, and deleting the
  tagged one could then lose the row. The design's other branch, delete and reinsert by a
  descent, is not built: a scan reads one page at a time, so moving an
  entry to another page could make it miss the row or return it twice.
* '''Split points.''' A leaf split cuts a run of one row's entries only
  when the page has no other split point. The runs are one row's same-key
  versions between two VACUUMs, so tagged pivots, and flagged pages, come
  only from a page that is all one run.
* '''CREATE INDEX (T1, 2026-10-22).''' The build sorts tagged rows with the
  rest, in a tuplesort variant of BARK's own (<code>bark_sort_begin</code>)
  ordered by (key, TID, tag); nbtree's sort breaks ties on
  <code>t_tid</code>, which a tagged SINGLE uses for its trailer.
  <code>bark_load</code> packs tagged SINGLEs and never coalesces them. A
  leaf the load would end inside a run of one row's entries moves the run's
  part to the next leaf, unless the part fills the page or takes more than
  a quarter of it; then the high key keeps a tag and the next leaf gets
  <code>BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED</code>, as after a split. Only rows too large
  to sort take the second pass, as tagged OVERSIZED entries. Release
  build, 1M rows on <code>heap_versioned</code>, median of 3:
{| class="wikitable"
! tagged !! workers !! before: time, pages !! after: time, pages
|-
| 0% || 0 || 296 ms, 2737 || 291 ms, 2737
|-
| 0% || 4 || 176 ms, 2737 || 175 ms, 2737
|-
| 50% || 0 || 1187 ms, 5470 || 333 ms, 3829
|-
| 50% || 4 || 1081 ms, 5470 || 198 ms, 3829
|-
| 100% || 0 || 1714 ms, 5425 || 341 ms, 4922
|-
| 100% || 4 || 1610 ms, 5425 || 194 ms, 4922
