  The keys are distinct int4s. At 100% tagged the build now takes 1.17x
  the untagged time serial and 1.11x with 4 workers, against 5.8x and 9.2x
  before. The index is 1.8x the untagged size, against 2.0x before: a
  tagged SINGLE of an int4 is 32 bytes (key, then heap TID and tag in its
  trailer), an untagged one 16.
* '''Deletion (R3.3).''' Simple and bottom-up deletion hand the table AM
  each entry's tag in <code>TM_IndexStatus</code>, a TID once per entry.
  Before a leaf of an index that stores tags splits, BARK offers its tagged
  entries to <code>table_index_delete_tuples</code>, so stale entries of
  live rows go.
* '''CIC''' needs nothing more: validation's bulk delete hands the version
  callback each entry's (TID, tag).
* '''Checks.''' The page validator and amcheck check the tagged shapes,
  the pivot rule, the (key, TID, tag) order, no tag in an index that does
  not store them, and that a leaf whose lower bound has a tag has the flag.
  pageinspect shows tags and the flag.

''Tests'' (the table AM side, <code>test_versioned_tableam</code>'s
<code>heap_versioned</code>, whose tag is a version's raw xmin, lives on a
test branch and is not to be merged): tagged inserts and builds, serial and
parallel; the tag on every scan path and at the unique checks; split
points, the flag, its propagation, untagging in place, and the order
violation that untagging without the rule causes (an injection point turns
the rule off); LP_DEAD on tagged entries; the pre-split cleanup; CIC with
heapallindexed; OVERSIZED tagged entries; crash recovery; a random
sequence of updates and VACUUMs. All of them run under
<code>wal_consistency_checking</code> with a standby. The validator's own
test plants a zero tag.

* '''Lowering a lower bound (T2, 2026-10-23).''' After its scan, VACUUM
  lowers a tagged lower bound (K, L, t0) of a page whose first entry it kept
  tagged to (K, L) with no tag (<code>bark_lower_bound</code>): the left
  sibling's high key and the parent's downlink, once the left page holds no
  entry of (K, L), and clears the page's flag, then untags the entry in
  place. No entry moves; a reader that read the old bound moves right from
  the left page's new high key, as after a split. Locks as page deletion's,
  the parent first and both leaves tried without waiting. Logged as the
  second form of <code>XLOG_BARK_UNLINK_PAGE</code>
  (<code>XLH_BARK_UNLINK_LOWER_SEP</code>, no block 0), since the rmgr has no
  free opcode. It declines, and a later VACUUM retries, when a lock is busy,
  the left page split since the scan, or the run still has a live entry on
  the left page; and stays declined when the page is its parent's first
  child (the bound is then also a separator a level up), an OVERSIZED bound,
  or an empty left page whose own lower bound is in the run.

''Follow-ups.'' T2 leaves a tag whose bound is a parent's first child's,
about once per parent fan-out of tagged bounds; lowering it at the upper
levels too would clear every tag, if a table AM needs that
(xid-wraparound). V2: versioned multikey indexes.

''V2 conversion (a tagged multikey index made unique or exclusion after its
build).'' The design is section 12.1 of the V2 design report; README
"Conversion" has the as-built rules. Item by item:
* Item 5, done (this commit): until the conversion settles, a non-MVCC
  scan of such an index (a constraint check) keeps no row per locator but
  the one this backend inserted last, and rechecks every row, so it meets
  every version an in-progress in-place update left at a locator. Tests:
  isolation <code>bark_mk_convert</code> (T12b; a duplicate committed
  before), <code>bark_mk_versioned</code> (T12c and the dirty-scan cases).
  Limit: a check of a row other than the last inserted can raise "found
  self tuple multiple times"; an amapi hint carrying the self TID would
  lift it.
* Items 1-2, done (this commit): meta bits <code>BARK_META_CONVERTING</code>
  (1 << 3) and <code>BARK_META_SETTLED</code> (1 << 4). The first BARK call
  that sees MKTAGS and a unique or exclusion rd_index sets CONVERTING
  (<code>bark_note_conversion</code>: insert, a constraint check's rescan,
  VACUUM), logged as a meta page image; never in recovery. Item 3 as
  built: CONVERTING makes <code>amindexmaykeeplocator</code> false even
  after the constraint is dropped; <code>amindexcanversion</code> stays
  MKTAGS (the design had it drop with CONVERTING), since an update that
  began before the conversion and keeps its locator must still get tagged
  entries. amcheck and the validator (primary and redo) report CONVERTING
  without MKTAGS and SETTLED without CONVERTING (T12f in
  <code>008_bark_mktags.pl</code>). The bits are checked again under the
  meta page's lock: isolation <code>bark_mk_convert_race</code> stops a
  caller before it (another insert set CONVERTING; a VACUUM completed the
  conversion while a dirty scan waited).
* Items 4 and 7, done (this commit), with the amendment approved by the
  lead (2026-10-27): after SETTLED, BARK stores new entries of the index
  untagged (every writer then moves rows, and a new locator has no
  history), and a barrier that sets SETTLED waits once more, as does every
  later VACUUM before it counts, so that a writer that read the meta page
  before SETTLED was set is outwaited too. The item as written ("no tag
  added after SETTLED") did not hold: <code>amindexcanversion</code> stays
  MKTAGS, and the executor tags every entry of such an index, moved row or
  not. The barrier: <code>WaitForLockers</code>(table, ShareLock) in
  bulkdelete, or in cleanup when no bulkdelete of this transaction tried
  it; a parallel worker skips it; autovacuum uses
  <code>GetLockConflicts</code> and does not wait. The false answer of
  CONVERTING is read again in every transaction (the bit sends no
  invalidation). Completion: a pass after the barrier counts the tagged
  leaf entries it leaves (exactly: a deleting pass, cycle IDs); zero clears
  MKTAGS, CONVERTING and SETTLED and invalidates the relcache entry. Tagged
  pivots may remain and are accepted (a pivot tag needs only
  <code>BARK_META_VERSIONED</code>). Item 5's rule ends at SETTLED. Tests:
  T12a (<code>bark_mk_versioned</code>), T12b's VACUUM permutation,
  T12e (<code>009_bark_convert.pl</code>, wal_consistency_checking and a
  standby). Limits: an entry kept tagged (its page's lower bound, or a row
  the table AM never untags) keeps the conversion open; a table that always
  has a writer settles only under a manual VACUUM.
* Item 6 (per-leaf conversion): open.

=== C-MVCC: visibility, SSI, and the index-only / recheck path ===

BARK entries obey the same visibility rules as nbtree. Four obligations:

* '''Snapshot visibility and SSI.''' Scans acquire the same predicate locks
  and honor the same snapshot semantics as nbtree; a serialization-relevant
  read takes a predicate lock at the granularity nbtree would.
* '''A build serves snapshots older than itself.''' CREATE INDEX and REINDEX
  index every row the table AM passes, including those it marks not alive
  (RECENTLY_DEAD), which are kept only out of the uniqueness check, as
  nbtree's spool2 keeps them; nothing on the page records the fact
  (README "CREATE INDEX"; tested by <code>bark_recently_dead.spec</code>).
* '''Index-only scans need a leaf-key recheck that deduplicates.''' Re-running
  <code>indexqualorig</code> against the live tuple drops false positives but
  '''cannot''' dedup two index entries that chain-walk to the same live tuple
  (that returns duplicate rows), and per-scan TID dedup breaks
  <code>ORDER BY</code> (it yields the tuple at the stale key's sort
  position). The correct general mechanism is comparing the '''stored leaf
  key''': BARK must retain enough of the leaf key to do this.
* '''The recheck-hint bit.''' A proposed change to
  <code>236cff2f</code> (<code>src/include/storage/itemptr.h</code>).'' The
  tepid branch reserves bit 14 of a TID's offset for a locator whose match is
  not exact, and <code>tbm_add_tuples</code> turns such a TID's page lossy.
  ''Status:'' not in this tree.  Every BARK entry is exact, so BARK would
  never set the bit, and carrying it cost a mask in
  <code>ItemPointerCompare</code> and <code>ItemPointerGetOffsetNumber</code>
  for every caller; it was dropped from the series (2026-10-15) and stays on
  the tepid branch for the table AM that needs it.  BARK's rechecks go
  through <code>xs_recheck</code> and the recheck argument of
  <code>tbm_add_tuples</code>.
=== C-DELETE: delete-marking via UNDO ===

''Source: the UNDO patch set (<code>src/include/access/amapi.h</code>,
<code>access/nbtree.h</code>, <code>access/tableam.h</code>).''

An AM over an UNDO-backed table participates in index delete-marking rather
than leaving dead entries for VACUUM:

* <code>TableAmRoutine.am_supports_undo</code> gates the whole mechanism;
  <code>RelationUsesIndexUndo()</code> (the <code>index_undo</code> reloption)
  is the per-relation switch.
* <code>IndexAmRoutine.amdeletemark</code> / <code>amundomark</code> (both may
  be NULL for an AM that opts out) mark and un-mark an entry.
* nbtree's encoding in that patch set, which BARK mirrors: an alternate-TID
  subtype with <code>BT_IS_DELETE_MARKED = 0x4000</code> set (and
  <code>BT_IS_POSTING</code> clear) in the entry's <code>t_tid</code> offset.
  <code>genam.h</code>'s <code>index_delete_mark</code> /
  <code>index_undo_mark</code> dispatch to the AM hooks; the UNDO rmgr replays
  them.

'''BARK obligation:''' reserve a delete-marked entry subtype in the on-disk
format from the start, and implement <code>amdeletemark</code>/<code>amundomark</code>
when UNDO integration lands (not in the first functional AM).
''Status:'' none of that interface is in this tree (no
<code>amdeletemark</code>, <code>am_supports_undo</code>,
<code>BT_IS_DELETE_MARKED</code> or <code>index_delete_mark</code>).
The bit once reserved for it, 0x4000, is <code>BARK_HAS_TAG</code> since
version 3 (versioned locators: design section 5, "Version tags" in the
README), which designs index undo out (no index undo; abort leaves entries
the table AM's validity rule rejects).  Delete-marking, if it is ever
needed, takes a new code point.

=== C-KEYSHAPE: the key/value shape and the inverted paradigm ===

This is the contract that is native to BARK rather than inherited. It defines
the entry as a (key, value-set) pair where:

* the key is variable length, ordered by a B-tree-family operator class;
* the value side is one of: a single locator (the nbtree shape), a sorted
  list of locators (sorted duplicates, the libdb shape), or a locator '''set'''
  encoded as a posting list (the inverted shape, sbm-backed when clustered);
* a plain scalar index and an inverted index share one AM and one on-disk
  framework. No operator class declares shapes: an index uses LIST and
  POSTING entries when it is not unique, has no INCLUDE columns and every
  key column's equalimage procedure says yes
  (<code>bark_allequalimage</code>, nbtree's deduplication rule), and only
  SINGLE (and OVERSIZED) entries otherwise.

This is the contract that lets a future OrioleDB- or DocumentDB-style inverted
index be a BARK opclass instead of a separate AM. The encoding decision
(single / list / posting-set) is per entry and driven by size: one locator is
a SINGLE, more are a POSTING when that is smaller than the LIST, reserve
included (<code>bark_form_entry</code>), so sbm is used only where its
density envelope wins.

Removing locators must never make an entry grow, because VACUUM rewrites an
entry in place under the page's one WAL record (as nbtree shrinks a posting
list), with no split and no re-insert that a concurrent scan or a crash could
observe half done. A TID array (nbtree, LIST) and GIN's varbyte lists shrink
on removal by construction; an sbm does not, since removing members breaks
all-ones vectors and RLE runs into stored vectors. So a posting-set entry
reserves its worst case: it is sized for <code>sbm_removal_bound</code>, the
largest serialization any subset of its set can have, which is
non-increasing under removal. The reserve costs at most one bit per locator of
a dense run (8 bytes per 64 locators), and the posting-set shape is chosen only
when it beats the list shape with the reserve included.

==== Containment queries: in BARK, or beside it ====

"Which rows contain term X" is a key lookup in BARK: once a multikey class extracts a
row's terms, the tree maps each term to its locator set, in SINGLE, LIST and
POSTING entries over consecutive leaves, which is the shape GIN and pg_tre
build from a term tree and per-term posting trees. What BARK keeps in house,
and what it leaves to another index:

* '''Exact containment''' (an array or document holds X; all of X, Y; any
  of them) belongs in BARK. A single term is an equality scan. Several terms
  combine either as bitmap scans the executor ANDs and ORs, which works
  today, or inside one BARK scan: with heap TID order, a term's run is in TID order, so
  intersecting a rare term with a common one probes the common term's run
  at (term, TID) for each candidate instead of reading it (a leapfrog join,
  as a WAND cursor seeks), and two POSTING entries intersect as sbm sets
  without materializing either (<code>sbm_intersection</code>,
  <code>sbm_intersection_cardinality</code>).
* '''Ranked, positional and fuzzy text search''' (BM25, phrases, regex,
  edit distance) does not. It needs corpus statistics, per-occurrence data
  (term frequency, positions) and immutable, bulk-encoded posting blocks
  with impact metadata for block-max WAND, as pg_fts keeps in its segments,
  or trigram formulas resolved over posting sets and rechecked against the
  heap, as pg_tre does. Those designs trade in-place update for bulk
  rewrite, which a B-tree that promises ordered, lock-coupled reads cannot.
* '''Combining the two''' needs one thing core PostgreSQL lacks: an ordered
  index scan filtered by another index's candidate set without visiting the
  heap for the rows it rejects. <code>WHERE body @@@ q ORDER BY ts LIMIT
  10</code> today either sorts all of the text index's matches or reads BARK
  in order and checks every row in the heap. A TID filter on the scan
  (another index's result as a TID set that the scan consults before
  returning an item) gives the plan that touches only matching rows, in
  order. With <code>amgetbatch</code> the filter belongs in the batch layer,
  where it works for every AM; it is a core proposal, not a BARK format
  change.

The cost to watch in house is writes: a document with N distinct terms is N
inserts. The one-pass insert of a row's sorted keys amortizes the descents,
and bottom-up deletion and coalescing keep version churn from growing the index; a GIN-style
pending list is not an option, since ordered scans would have to merge it.

==== Value sets that outgrow one entry ====

The question this section answers: should BARK keep a large key's locator set
in a structure of its own (a B-tree per key, as GIN does, or WiredTiger-style
skiplists), rather than in entries of the main tree? It records what was
measured, the options, and the order in which to do the ones worth doing.
sbm stays the posting-set encoding throughout.

'''What BARK does today.''' An entry holds at most
<code>BarkMaxItemSize</code> (2712 bytes). When a key's LIST or POSTING entry
cannot grow, the next locator for that key becomes a new entry of the same
key, next to it in the main tree. BARK keys need not be unique, so the main
tree already does the job of GIN's posting tree: a key's entries sit on
consecutive leaves under ordinary pivots, a scan reads them in order, and
none of this has a per-locator cost (run 2: an index-only scan spends 90 ns
per locator on a key spread over 31 entries, and 94 ns on keys of one entry
each). GIN needs a separate tree only because
its entry tree holds each key once. The one thing a per-key tree would add is
locator order within a key. When this note was written, insert
coalesced into the last entry of the key's run, so the entries of a key were
in arrival order and their locator ranges could overlap. Since then
they are in heap TID order with disjoint ranges, which gives
that order without a second tree (option (b) below).

'''Measurements.''' All on a release build (<code>-Dcassert=false</code>),
one session, 8-CPU laptop, <code>shared_buffers=2GB</code>,
<code>synchronous_commit=off</code>, <code>fsync=off</code>. "Retail" means
the index exists before <code>INSERT ... SELECT</code>; "build" means
<code>CREATE INDEX</code> after the load. Datasets: ''one'' is 1M rows of a
single key; ''kc'' is 10M rows over 1000 keys, each key's rows inserted
together (clustered); ''ks'' is 10M rows over 1000 keys, round-robin
(scattered). <code>bark(a) INCLUDE (b)</code> never coalesces, so it is BARK
with only SINGLE entries. Runs 1 and 2 were taken on BARK before the
POSTING removal reserve; run 3 on the reserve commit; runs 4 and 5 on the
current tip, which adds byte- and fillfactor-based split points. Page counts
are deterministic. Times on this shared host vary by up to 2x with load from
other work, so each A/B row below alternates the two binaries on one data
directory, restarting the server between runs. The datasets are
<code>SELECT 1, (g/1000)%1000 FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g</code>
(''one''), <code>SELECT (g-1)/10000, g%1000 FROM generate_series(1,10000000) g</code>
(kc) and <code>SELECT g%1000, (g/1000)%1000 FROM generate_series(1,10000000) g</code>
(ks), loaded from an unlogged copy into <code>r (a int, b int)</code>.
