  <code>sbm_create_from_array</code>, which retail insert's general
  coalesce path also gets. A parallel build writes the same tree. Retail
  insert can still grow a built entry up to <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>.

'''Recommendation, in order.'''

# '''Drop the cardinality hint from <code>sbm_serialize</code>.''' One
  commit in <code>sbm.c</code>/<code>sbm.h</code>. Write 0 in bytes 8-15, or
  write the cached value only when it is already valid, and document the
  field as reserved. Either way the wire format does not change, so
  <code>SBM_WIRE_VERSION</code> stays. The <code>test_sbm</code>
  round-trip tests cover it, since nothing reads the field. Cuts insert
  time for POSTING appends by 21% (kc) to 31% (''one''). Changes no page or
  WAL byte, and touches no file other work is changing.
# '''Batch POSTING decode.''' One commit: <code>bark_posting_get_tids</code>
  decodes through <code>sbm_scan</code>, about 15 lines, which is the change
  measured above. Test: the POSTING differential tests in
  <code>bark.sql</code> already compare every scan mode with a sequential
  scan. Makes the POSTING bitmap node 6-10x faster and index-only scans
  35-40% faster. The page-at-a-time scan position also reads members
  through <code>bark_entry_get_tids</code>, so it gets the speedup without
  changes.
# '''Form LIST/POSTING at CREATE INDEX''' (option f), done. One commit in
  <code>barksort.c</code>, with a size test: build vs retail page counts
  within a factor, <code>bark_index_check</code> clean, index results equal
  to a sequential scan. Leaves stay packed to fillfactor, as now. The
  build's <code>bark_form_hikey</code> and <code>bark_form_downlink</code>
  truncate with <code>index_truncate_tuple</code>, which copies the whole
  tuple, body included, when the index has no INCLUDE columns. Once the
  build emits LIST and POSTING entries, pivots must be formed from the
  entry stripped to its key, as <code>bark_strip_to_key</code> does for
  insert-side splits. Suffix truncation's build path, which forms the pivot
  from lastleft and firstright, needs the same.
# '''The page-fill merge pass''' (option c), done in part. The pass,
  <code>bark_merge_page</code> in <code>barkdelete.c</code>, runs under the
  leaf's exclusive lock where <code>bark_insert</code> would call
  <code>bark_split</code>, nbtree's slot
  (<code>_bt_delete_or_dedup_one_page</code>, just before
  <code>_bt_split</code>): for an entry that does not fit, and for a heap TID
  inside a LIST or POSTING that can neither take it nor be divided in place.
  It merges adjacent entries of a key, in page order, into entries of up to
  <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>, and logs one <code>XLOG_BARK_MERGE</code>
  record. Scans copy a page's matches under the share lock, as
  <code>_bt_readpage</code> does, which is what makes the in-place rewrite
  safe, and VACUUM cycle IDs are not affected, because no entry moves to
  another page. It is added beside <code>bark_coalesce_list</code>, not in
  place of it, and is aimed at CREATE INDEX-built leaves, whose LISTs are
  capped at a tenth of a page: under churn their splits were 50/50 splits
  that a merge avoids (see "Merging a page's entries before a split" in the
  bark README). Still open: replacing the per-insert coalesce with SINGLE
  inserts merged by this pass, with a WAL-volume check on ks against the
  per-insert coalesce. Expected: ks WAL 11.6 GB down to about 2-3 GB, and
  insert time down to near SINGLE-only, measured on a quiet host.
# '''A BARK WAL rmgr''' (option e), as a series once the concurrency, VACUUM
  and split work has merged. The first commit is done: the "Bark" resource
  manager with <code>rm_mask</code>, and records for VACUUM (replayed under a
  cleanup lock), page deletion, freeing an overflow page and page reuse, whose
  replay resolves the hot-standby conflict that page recycling needs
  (<code>027_stream_regress</code> passes under
  <code>wal_consistency_checking=all</code>; see "WAL" in the bark README).
  The second adds the insert records (<code>INSERT_LEAF</code>,
  <code>INSERT_UPPER</code>, and <code>OVERWRITE</code> for a grown LIST or
  POSTING entry). Release build, 1M retail inserts, three rounds:
  unique keys 2.7-2.9 s to 1.27 s and 167 to 151 MB of WAL; 1000 clustered
  keys (POSTING) 3.3-3.7 s to 1.74 s, but 481 to 705 MB; 1000 scattered keys
  (LIST) 6.3-6.9 s to 2.44 s and 1782 to 1282 MB. <code>OVERWRITE</code> logs
  the whole new entry, which a LIST grows up to the 2712-byte item limit, and
  an sbm add changes fewer bytes than the entry holds. The third commit logs
  only the added locator (<code>ADD_TID</code>, as nbtree's
  <code>INSERT_POST</code>; redo re-forms the entry): POSTING appends 1.74 to
  1.24 s and 705 to 121 MB (481 MB with generic WAL), LIST appends 2.44 to
  1.42 s and 1282 to 129 MB (1782 MB with generic WAL). The fourth logs
  splits, new roots and an empty index's first root (<code>SPLIT</code>,
  <code>NEWROOT</code>, <code>CREATE_ROOT</code>): each split half is logged
  as its packed entries and rebuilt in redo, with no page image. The fifth
  logs overflow chains (<code>OVERFLOW</code>), the last generic WAL: every
  change BARK makes after CREATE INDEX is now in a Bark record.

Not recommended: a per-key posting tree (b) and a pending list (d), for the
reasons above. If C-DELETE or retail delete later needs to find one
(key, locator) pair, make the locator a tie-breaking key attribute in
pivots, as nbtree did in version 4, rather than adding a second tree.

=== C-STATS: statistics and cost ===

BARK feeds the planner the same statistics contract as nbtree:
<code>amcostestimate</code> produces comparable correlation, per-tuple, and
startup costs; <code>ANALYZE</code> sees index expressions the same way; the
planner's ordered-path generation (see the sweep below) treats a BARK scalar
index as interchangeable with a B-tree for costing.

barkcostestimate is btcostestimate applied to the quals BARK's scan uses to
position and stop (no IS NULL bound, equality arrays on the equality prefix
in either direction with one descent per combination of their elements,
skipping only over column 1), with the index correlation
from <code>btcost_correlation</code>, plus a charge for overflow chains.
It counts the entries a scan reads in heap rows, as nbtree does after
deduplication; LIST and POSTING entries lower the cost through the index's
smaller page count. For the same reason VACUUM reports the index's live
heap TIDs as its tuple count, not its entries, and a cleanup-only scan's
count is an estimate that leaves <code>reltuples</code> alone.

=== The capability sweep ===

There were '''30''' <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code> references in the tree when this
was written, and 33 when the sweep was done, in <code>.c</code> and <code>.h</code>
files outside the catalog headers. They fall into three groups; only the
middle group is BARK work.

# '''Must stay nbtree (policy).''' Catalog and TOAST index creation
  (<code>catalog/toasting.c</code>), and the opclass/opfamily lookup helpers
  that answer "what is the btree opclass for this type"
  (<code>typcache.c</code>, <code>lsyscache.c</code>, <code>typecmds.c</code>,
  <code>opclasscmds.c</code>, <code>amvalidate.c</code>,
  <code>nbtvalidate.c</code>, <code>parse_clause.c</code>). These legitimately
  mean the B-tree operator family, not "the only ordered AM," and are left
  alone.
# '''The sweep target: "btree is the only thing that can produce ordered
  paths / be sorted on / infer an ON CONFLICT arbiter."''' The planner and
  executor sites -- <code>optimizer/path/indxpath.c</code>,
  <code>optimizer/util/plancat.c</code>,
  <code>utils/sort/tuplesortvariants.c</code> (the
  <code>Assert(relam == BTREE_AM_OID)</code>), <code>commands/repack.c</code>,
  <code>utils/adt/ri_triggers.c</code>, <code>commands/tablecmds.c</code> --
  are rewritten to ask an AM capability ("can produce ordered scans",
  "supports this sort") rather than to compare against
  <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code>. This is the largest mechanical part of the series.
  ''Status:'' the planner sites are done by the
  <code>ambtreeopfamilies</code> capability (
  <code>plancat.c</code>'s sort-family fast path,
  <code>match_rowcompare_to_indexcol</code>,
  <code>ec_member_matches_indexcol</code>). Still btree-only:
  <code>tuplesort_begin_cluster</code> (its Assert), so
  <code>repack.c</code> never sorts for a BARK index; the RI fast path in
  <code>ri_triggers.c</code>, which falls back to SPI for other AMs; and
  <code>tablecmds.c</code>'s partition opclass choice, which is a btree
  opclass lookup and stays. <code>pg_amcheck</code> now checks BARK indexes
  too.
# '''Ambiguous, decided case by case''' (<code>access/index/amapi.c</code>'s
  index-AM validation): keep the btree-specific validation for btree, add
  BARK's own.

KNN ordering (<code>amcanorderbyop</code>) was a reserved field in BARK's
<code>IndexAmRoutine</code> from the first commit and was implemented later;
<code>indxpath.c</code> gives an index that is both
ordered and <code>amcanorderbyop</code> at most one ordering operator, on its
leading column.

