  high-key estimate, the merge's target, the inline limit); the version tag
  grows it from 8 to 16 bytes in one line. RethinkDB's
  <code>leaf_node.cc</code> documents the bug it avoids: an optional field
  counted in one size calculation and not another. A deterministic
  workload's 3495 pages were byte-identical before and after.
* ''R1, the page validator.'' amcheck's checks of one page moved to
  <code>barkverify.c</code> (<code>bark_page_verify</code>), grouped SHAPES
  (no catalog, no comparator), ORDER, CONTENTS and COMPLETE; amcheck calls
  it, its messages unchanged. In cassert builds every function that ends a
  critical section on a BARK page runs SHAPES and ORDER on each page it
  changed, after the critical section and under the lock, and WAL redo runs
  SHAPES on each page it replays. A failure is an ERROR in a backend (the
  change is logged, so a PANIC would only replay it again) and a PANIC in
  redo. From RethinkDB's <code>leaf::validate</code>, run after every node
  mutation in its debug builds; sqlxtc's port caught a planted one-byte
  fault its own test oracle missed. The developer option
  <code>bark_verify_pages</code> (cassert builds only, on by default) turns
  it off. A change of one or a few adjacent entries checks those entries
  (and their order against the entry on each side), and every 32nd such
  check in a backend the whole page; other changes check the whole page;
  redo checks one such record in 32, whole, and every other record in
  full. t/015_page_validator.pl plants a LIST count fault and an order
  fault next to an ADD_TID (the INSERT fails at the call site; with the
  validator off only amcheck reports the page; the standby's redo PANICs),
  one away from it (a later change's whole-page check reports it), and
  faults in a meta page and an overflow page. No false positive in the
  BARK suites, core regress and isolation, or 027_stream_regress with
  wal_consistency_checking. Cost under cassert: 5.6x on a long-LIST write
  workload with every page checked whole, 1.9x bounded, 1.5x with the
  shapes of the touched entries only; 1.17x on the BARK suites (README,
  "Page validator").
* ''R5, VACUUM's read stream.'' nbtree's c5c239e26e3 ported to
  <code>bark_vacuum_scan</code>: a <code>block_range_read_stream_cb</code>
  stream over the data blocks, re-armed for blocks added during the scan;
  backtracking keeps <code>ReadBufferExtended</code>. Measured on
  c7i.4xlarge (gp3 EBS, release build, shared_buffers 128MB, server
  restarted and OS cache dropped before each timed VACUUM, PARALLEL 0;
  10M rows, 30% deleted, four indexes (k), (d), (h), (v); median of 5):
  VACUUM's reads in pg_stat_io fell from 142,195 to 19,849 for the same
  2.6 GB, but the time did not: BARK 17.7 s before and 18.2 s after,
  nbtree 18.6 s and 18.7 s, bark/btree 0.95 and 0.97 (pairwise 0.93-0.96
  and 0.95-0.98). The heap passes dominate, and the volume serves 8 kB
  reads in order well enough that combining them gains nothing here; the
  win, if any, is on slower or contended storage.
* ''R4a, orphaned overflow chains.'' <code>bark_index_parent_check</code>
  follows every OVERSIZED entry's chain, leaf entries', high keys' and
  downlinks', and reports loops, crossed chains, chains that reach a
  non-overflow page or are longer than their entry, and, with a WARNING,
  overflow pages no chain reaches. Two leaks it found are fixed: VACUUM's
  deletion of an empty leaf now frees the chain of the OVERSIZED downlink
  it removes, which only the deleted leaf's high key shared, and an insert
  that writes an oversized key's chain on the fast path's cached leaf and
  then gives the leaf up frees that chain. Orphans are now left only by a
  crash, or an error between writing a chain and placing its entry;
  reclaiming them is R4c.
